{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792351223,"nonce":"PT68y3nNdmoB3eBF6bA12WJH+Yw=","title":"App icon on every platform: assets, packaging, and the runtime Dock icon","message":"Done\n- assets/rayde-icon.png (the user's) is the source. scripts/icons.sh derives assets/icons: rayde.icns (the Big Sur grid, with a shadow), rayde.ico (seven sizes, 32-bit PNG entries), the Linux hicolor tree (16 to 1024 px) and rayde-256.rgba. The script and its output are committed; only a change to the icon needs ImageMagick.\n- The 256 px icon is embedded in the program (rayde.ui.AppIcon) and passed as WindowConfig.icon: shown on Windows and most Linux windows and taskbars. Checked: size, corner and middle alpha, the .ico directory, the .icns header, every hicolor size.\n- macOS: scripts/package-macos.sh builds RayDE.app (Info.plist, icns, program, natives, rayscript-host, launcher). Run once on the extension fixture: the window opened, and the Studio started the extension.\n- Linux: scripts/package-linux.sh installs the launcher, rayde.desktop, MIME types for .hxml and .rayext, and the hicolor icons.\n- Windows: packaging/windows/RayDE.cmd and install-shortcut.ps1.\n\nOpen\n- macOS, run from source: winit ignores window icons, so the Dock shows ash's icon. Asked of ashui-8c: set the application icon image from WindowConfig.icon (hlwindow native, NSApplication setApplicationIconImage).\n- The Linux and Windows packaging has not been run on those systems.\n- macOS: the app is not signed or notarised; double-clicking a .hxml or .rayext in Finder does not open it (file associations need an Apple Event handler, which a shell launcher cannot have).\n- A launcher without ash says so and stops; ash could be bundled.","files":null}]}